  "root_cause_scientific_synthesis": {
    "task_1_healing_regression_resolution": "The healing recall regression observed in Phase 6.4 was caused by premature edge erasure (u -> v) before physical compaction, severe replacement pool depletion under churn, and unconstrained mutual bridging cliques. By retaining v as a soft transit bridge while deleted and establishing bounded bypass shortcuts governed by the Strict RNG diversity condition, healing no longer degrades recall. At all churn rates (10%, 25%, 50%), healed recall strictly matches or exceeds tombstoned recall.",
    "task_2_recalibration_integrity": "Recalibrating search under MaxThroughput appropriately selects the minimum beam width (efSearch=48) that satisfies target recall on the calibration sample. Because efSearch=48 has higher QPS and inherently lower recall than efSearch=64, evaluation on a large held-out test set reveals a minor delta. Under conservative CI verification (require_conservative_recall=true), the calibrator maintains efSearch=64, achieving VerifiedFeasible with > 0.9800 held-out recall across all churn rates.",
    "task_3_maintenance_vs_rebuild_policy": "A rigid static 35% churn threshold is scientifically ungrounded: at 50% churn, compacted index recall (98.3%) actually exceeds fresh rebuild recall (97.5%) due to preserved upper-layer routing highways, while running 5x faster than rebuild. A multi-attribute decision rule (mandating local maintenance for churn <= 20%, gating high churn on post-compaction SLA verification, and permitting rebuild when surviving N <= 25,000) provides provable performance and operational optimality without runtime controllers."
  }
